course: dictionary
lesson: 14649
title: Formal words: preclude
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Preclude is a formal word. It means to stop something from happening, or to make it impossible. You will often find it in rules, contracts, news reports and exam questions. «A broken leg precluded him from running in the race.» The broken leg is the clue: he could not run, so precluded must mean stopped or made impossible.
explain: Preclude often has a person and a "from" after it. The pattern is preclude someone from doing something: «The rule precludes children from using the lift alone.» Children cannot use the lift alone, so preclude means stop or prevent.
explain: When you meet preclude, try an everyday word in its place: stop, prevent or rule out. «Heavy snow precluded the bus from reaching the village» means heavy snow prevented the bus from reaching the village. If the sentence still makes sense, you have found the meaning.
fact: Preclude means to prevent something from happening, or to make it impossible.
fact: Preclude someone from doing something means to stop them from doing it.
fact: Preclude is a verb: preclude, precludes, precluded, precluding.
fact: «Precluded» means stopped or made impossible, as in «The storm precluded a sailing trip».
fact: Preclude is formal. Everyday words for it are stop, prevent and rule out.
exercise: «A broken leg precluded him from running in the race.» What does precluded mean? | Made it faster | Cheered him on | *Made it impossible | Helped him to win
exercise: Which everyday word fits best in place of preclude? «The new rule precludes phones in class.» | Decorates | Rewards | *Stops | Invites
exercise: The pattern preclude someone from doing something means | Give someone a prize | Ask someone to do something | Watch someone do something | *Stop someone from doing something
test: «Heavy snow precluded the bus from reaching the village.» What does precluded mean? | *Prevented | Cleaned | Painted | Hurried
test: Which is the closest everyday meaning of preclude? | *Rule out | Ask for | Pass by | Forget
exam: «Her lack of practice precluded her from winning, the coach said.» What does precluded mean here? | Helped her win | Made her practise more | Gave her a medal | *Prevented her from winning
